#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b55178-7b17-5daa-a50a-e61909e400c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348498} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcefa06-9c35-5e7a-bdd9-cd95569d5d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348849} {:answer/id #uuid "78251345-0ae7-5da6-a30a-59130f191b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349551} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2cef54-71fa-5e98-8cf8-a1836a57cadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349594} {:answer/id #uuid "cc61605e-f687-5e68-bead-a350ce0987c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d2cef54-71fa-5e98-8cf8-a1836a57cadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349594} :answer-field/id #uuid "706695d8-950f-5334-ae12-d9c3e1bbf6eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5521ee3b-fffd-5558-98b1-b81f3a08c9ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-81164" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x} & {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} \\\\ {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & -x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,1)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$\n- The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}e & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=e\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=-{e}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=e>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76db4ced-1050-554a-aa89-def0f8a958f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349540} {:answer/id #uuid "8079a5d7-6a80-5567-b022-0e61db7ad867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349625} {:answer/id #uuid "9c302c53-4369-5ef0-9f10-d1fbc6f5c457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349839} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7da852-292c-5624-9a82-0c84441d9b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350543} {:answer/id #uuid "f61abcc5-138c-5d6e-ba74-df862169a892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f61abcc5-138c-5d6e-ba74-df862169a892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350602} :answer-field/id #uuid "60622f98-8769-5354-9360-49048591f1ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43f0f1fc-04b9-5788-b436-d0d8e07500dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-81375" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-{e}^{-{a}^{2}}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{0}+{e}^{-{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b8fde55-e83c-5be8-a243-bc3e0d4f1b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348984} {:answer/id #uuid "4abdc8b8-7340-5441-8247-cd9710e6d8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349128} {:answer/id #uuid "5e840f97-66fa-5708-ac1e-dada9cd5903f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349314} {:answer/id #uuid "65ba8b41-d7c2-5423-aa89-3bf70d806ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349376} {:answer/id #uuid "ba050e08-5f23-5543-a117-9239f86d3514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba050e08-5f23-5543-a117-9239f86d3514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350096} :answer-field/id #uuid "62635290-ba66-5211-8758-a27e4bc0bd64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e8066ca0-5e8f-5876-924e-8be55bf1f74e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82290" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- One of the rows/columns of $A$ is proportional to another row/column of $A$\n- $A$ has two identical rows\n- $A$ has a zero row" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=4\\cdot 3-6\\cdot 2=0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither two identical rows nor a zero row.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5296886d-83f6-5207-aa0e-249133775289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352306} {:answer/id #uuid "71fa7f3c-30df-5f38-90c0-d74a55991f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352564} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe59b78-afe6-5de3-9dbe-c3e1b24702f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352687} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5d5e24-d948-5d3f-9605-3630df2175e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352904} {:answer/id #uuid "ce58677c-af29-56aa-8e31-490d8a8ec48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fe59b78-afe6-5de3-9dbe-c3e1b24702f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352687} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3b4ff7e-6157-56ca-86d7-6ffe26a2b6b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6582765-66f3-5651-9e94-833ca793221c" :question/math-academy-id "q-84347" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $P?$\n\n- ${P}^{T}$ is orthogonal\n- ${P}^{-1}$ is **not** orthogonal\n- $-P$ is orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T} & ={P}^{T}\\cdot P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the product ${P}^{-1}\\cdot ({P}^{-1})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1}\\cdot ({P}^{-1})^{T} & ={P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={P}^{T}P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product $(-P)\\cdot (-P)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-P)\\cdot (-P)^{T} & =(-P)\\cdot (-{P}^{T}) \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33df2538-76f2-5cb0-a429-51ca8a7e976e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352050} {:answer/id #uuid "49a12346-021f-5652-a152-6325cd028a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352243} {:answer/id #uuid "669a23c9-af19-5731-993a-730dab6854e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352473} {:answer/id #uuid "7c12daba-184f-588b-9a66-0606d01a4943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352650} {:answer/id #uuid "e137dab9-7684-50dc-b9d3-4406e05d0fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33df2538-76f2-5cb0-a429-51ca8a7e976e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352050} :answer-field/id #uuid "142f11e6-6c5b-5f2f-b416-ebdf84bfdb31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55240a89-c0cc-59a9-b892-0f2b31965d95" :question/math-academy-id "q-84352" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\n- $5Q$ is also orthogonal\n- If $A=QB{Q}^{T}$ then $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$\n- ${Q}^{3}$ is the zero matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $5Q\\cdot 5{Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5Q\\cdot (5Q)^{T} & =5Q\\cdot 5{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =25Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =25I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(QB{Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(Q)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's consider the identity matrix\n\n$$\nQ=I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that $Q$ is an orthogonal matrix and also ${Q}^{3}=Q\\ne O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30460c98-d31d-594b-8b7c-68d7db0cafdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352009} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf219a0-b95d-5611-b2dd-adafaf4e5378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352522} {:answer/id #uuid "7069aab4-27eb-5e1a-9128-7fc186cc5530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352554} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf4a37e-8cd1-55b4-bad5-44ffa6ae4754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352794} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a780f1-4dce-565c-9007-c2e4dc9f03a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3a780f1-4dce-565c-9007-c2e4dc9f03a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353698} :answer-field/id #uuid "04ab3e40-1b7b-5725-a424-443b8aa544da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "020f4792-4587-5aa1-be12-a9175f71b2e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86867" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n- ${∬}_{R}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n- ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/62/62925844de27c9731b181f17a1bdd912bfa3265176bd62ebda425d182ae8d9e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not completely cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e5ec434-f4cc-58d6-8375-5045afef983d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355062} {:answer/id #uuid "678275ea-dd43-5baf-8492-544d1c4e25e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355580} {:answer/id #uuid "895dfc7b-06df-596d-947f-a61405e38d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355879} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7f3da4-39d2-55c1-a329-42904c091fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356191} {:answer/id #uuid "d2756f7b-7d85-5b1a-ac51-e8e534694163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e5ec434-f4cc-58d6-8375-5045afef983d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355062} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc827be4-abee-55e4-9080-2f2a9e51d077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72dda5b9-2988-5be6-b882-d73d509880d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-89885" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x+y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(-1,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(-1,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-1,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =1+(x+y) \\\\  & =x+y+1 \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y+1)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(-1,1).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)}>0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a6cdb6-ab0f-524d-97bd-06db3b5a52d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354858} {:answer/id #uuid "69ed6f41-3d3c-53cb-9ea9-7cfc50b14d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355599} {:answer/id #uuid "94991a27-5b8c-5e02-bf08-d97250d587f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355975} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc5cd95-75cc-5d50-8896-b8a83f6ee290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356452} {:answer/id #uuid "f1579c3f-f422-5b4d-84fe-dfeacf244db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccc5cd95-75cc-5d50-8896-b8a83f6ee290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356452} :answer-field/id #uuid "33fcd29d-a662-5511-8d0f-9fc1d521690e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21b379b1-3944-5c72-96df-e324e7834ad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-89886" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y-x)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1 \\\\  & =(y-x)-1 \\\\  & =y-x-1 \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y-x-1)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =0-0-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(0,0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,0)}<0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local maximum (not a local minimum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f01ea1-6eda-520d-9949-2a82df87eea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354720} {:answer/id #uuid "06f8c907-4bd4-5234-bbbe-f7d8d4f7cc68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354722} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd28fc3-f195-55e4-9e56-d3bd8503ebcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354905} {:answer/id #uuid "428a89c6-e679-5c9d-9997-04acb072b9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355252} {:answer/id #uuid "8b667bce-9485-516e-ab6c-f845955aa1ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "428a89c6-e679-5c9d-9997-04acb072b9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355252} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbfde428-b173-5e67-8b24-02d3788dff4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d8694a2-ea00-53e6-abdb-fa32dda7bdcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-89889" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(1,4)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(1,4)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,4),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}-(4) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+6x+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(-{x}^{3}+6x+y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =-(1)^{3}+6(1)+(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(1,4).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,4).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c0554f-d3fb-586d-a88a-903777e844ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354894} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4be897-5b57-5c8a-a0a6-b05565f4cfc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354916} {:answer/id #uuid "3e18e675-ea46-53bb-b370-b58c497d6555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355220} {:answer/id #uuid "81060160-6b44-5fec-9c4d-97fc18eef71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355797} {:answer/id #uuid "d40060af-72db-563b-9ca7-597aa16d8fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d40060af-72db-563b-9ca7-597aa16d8fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356517} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0428336-2bbd-5def-9dd0-f62ba311e200" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd483d10-bcb0-5d3a-aec6-2a397c70d709" :question/math-academy-id "q-90699" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+16y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡4x+{sin}^{2}⁡4x) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6b5f63-aaf9-5bf3-95e6-9b8950c0ebf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355030} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6e07e2-1fd4-5462-93f1-8c5de91cae56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355043} {:answer/id #uuid "3e42fc49-b62b-5190-987e-6600bd37b53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355222} {:answer/id #uuid "46a572ac-6f34-573c-8869-c7d78b9613bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355289} {:answer/id #uuid "f0132a2c-0556-5640-9497-faf83c24096e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46a572ac-6f34-573c-8869-c7d78b9613bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355289} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd674765-5c42-5338-b573-bc8ba66d6d7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21aff269-b5d0-5ff1-9c3e-386be14a7e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-94287" :question/problem "A particle of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=24\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{6}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{2}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =2$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17920bc3-3b7e-5099-87bc-91f0890de4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354876} {:answer/id #uuid "50fc65d4-669e-5ee6-ab6a-9a016a3d0703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355386} {:answer/id #uuid "718cc4af-ab18-5c33-91dc-2366149f11ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355666} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e67558-ffcf-50f5-b7ad-431e6880fea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356414} {:answer/id #uuid "d04b8244-2acd-5697-ae90-8069de899546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "718cc4af-ab18-5c33-91dc-2366149f11ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355666} :answer-field/id #uuid "74851fbd-2c2f-57a2-86d5-0a1706c0c9ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "de4a4e60-2947-5dae-80ca-6bd02414f29e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94288" :question/problem "A particle of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=21\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+3y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ),$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{21}{7}=3\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\sqrt{3}$ and ${ϕ}_{1}=ϕ$ in form 2 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2967b32a-c63e-5c49-9be1-0b312bf4e110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355009} {:answer/id #uuid "9cabe3c5-61f9-5a37-b58a-930b3dcbddd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356059} {:answer/id #uuid "a180d161-9846-5915-acd2-1e72bb38f841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356099} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1bd449-ec1d-562c-b250-d8aedf3d49f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356459} {:answer/id #uuid "d1dc1545-d354-5c14-be22-e75d302df324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2967b32a-c63e-5c49-9be1-0b312bf4e110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355009} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a73dfb-d874-53f6-bddb-ac500704fb9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8e4b9d3-5113-5f39-9a2d-eee9d1949dd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94310" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n- ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+4y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+4y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(4) & =33>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29809ee8-292b-5c9c-913b-0bb723fc7ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355010} {:answer/id #uuid "3ac74971-63d8-5b23-8fcf-97c3ece1fcb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355194} {:answer/id #uuid "3c628be3-3714-5241-9233-0637686d6ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355205} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ddee6f-5ab0-5a93-9234-09c063951125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356167} {:answer/id #uuid "b501d685-a302-5692-a5b9-3dc90442ee5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c628be3-3714-5241-9233-0637686d6ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355205} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd55c365-f91e-5a88-be8e-7db91b2b6861" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6276abb-11ab-5f2c-8db5-0bce52daa784" :question/math-academy-id "q-94311" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +7=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a71eb77-c12c-5cec-bf11-0e5015bd9045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355330} {:answer/id #uuid "58d37840-4038-5001-bec7-7d59f6b97472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355452} {:answer/id #uuid "72b3d522-e125-5325-b715-b212bf875da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355674} {:answer/id #uuid "76b2efa3-bff5-54ff-b62d-1e854ea6e203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355705} {:answer/id #uuid "f08bc086-5017-5952-b23a-dc70bf9be510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b2efa3-bff5-54ff-b62d-1e854ea6e203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355705} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df2e1f8-1c96-5da0-8938-5d076e74e024" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2840cb37-803e-5355-a4ea-a188990899c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94313" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+14y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}-4(1)(2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +9=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =13>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+14y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +14=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(14) & =-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2439d162-c1a7-50dd-be17-052a6b25c8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354968} {:answer/id #uuid "4f4068fd-c326-57d2-be53-fa7da7f90186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355373} {:answer/id #uuid "59449265-6d91-52d5-85e9-4e540a910284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355455} {:answer/id #uuid "b7be5d4f-138b-5028-a41f-bbe4b335f3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356274} {:answer/id #uuid "e74b95f2-282e-591b-b3b7-d3a72e5d6d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7be5d4f-138b-5028-a41f-bbe4b335f3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356274} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3b9a391-12d7-53d2-94ba-b63d5ec5be7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f4ed1b6-671a-54be-9f7b-f836039c55ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-94380" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[-5,0]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-5,0]\\to R$ and its subset $B={f\\in C[-5,0]\\,|\\,f(0)=0}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under function addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of $C[-5,0]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-5,0]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is  true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in B,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x)\\in B$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(0) & =kf(0) \\\\  & =k\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $B$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}]}
